            {
                "entry_id": "81e8f531-f4bd-321d-be60-a8d75529c3b8",
                "word": "darwin",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d5168e6d-4bf1-462a-b148-7cc62e89c2ec",
                        "entry_id": "81e8f531-f4bd-321d-be60-a8d75529c3b8",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "darwins"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "789dd190-d06a-3737-afaa-0fcbf30db8df",
                        "entry_id": "81e8f531-f4bd-321d-be60-a8d75529c3b8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A unit of evolutionary change in evolutionary biology."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "18a2b1be-8e37-4940-82f5-46b60c32518d",
                                "sense_id": "789dd190-d06a-3737-afaa-0fcbf30db8df",
                                "text": "One darwin (d) equals the rate of evolution that would produce a change in size by a factor of approximately 2.7 in one million years."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a6bf2ee5-7bd7-42e4-86de-70083434bad6",
                                "sense_id": "789dd190-d06a-3737-afaa-0fcbf30db8df",
                                "text": "The rate of evolutionary change in a species' wing or leg or beak (remember Darwin's finches mentioned in Chapter 1?) is assessed in degree-of-physical-change units called darwins."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "829f22bc-0e13-4ec4-bb2b-f173779b849e",
                                "sense_id": "789dd190-d06a-3737-afaa-0fcbf30db8df",
                                "text": "For instance, gradual change is relatively common in vertebrates (about .08 darwins), though some rapidly evolving vertebrates lineages show rates as high as 10 darwins, over short periods."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
        ],
